{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012xK8vwZgADKRCkRCgxQNZ7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review evaluates the \"worst\" scenario paper (negative results) across all requested dimensions:\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Evidence-strength stratification (AUROC gradient from 0.811 weak to 0.698 strong) is a genuine methodological contribution that exposes confounds in prior work\n- Direction stability analysis with cosine tracking, held-out AUROC, and recalibration events is thorough\n- R1-only ablation demonstrating reward hacking (34 instances) is a valuable cautionary finding\n- Statistical testing is honest: all pairwise p-values reported, no cherry-picking\n- Comprehensive baseline coverage spanning the relevant design space\n- Three-way decomposition (cosine = -0.82) provides structural geometric insight\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n- Single model (Qwen3-8B) with single LoRA config; the \"fundamental limitation\" claim is overstated for n=1\n- Recalibration strategy is underexplored (only one approach tested), so direction instability could be an engineering problem rather than inherent\n- R1-R3 disagreement analysis has circularity: low disagreement could reflect R1's degraded accuracy rather than absence of masked sycophancy\n- Manual review sample too small (n=50, only 8 positives) for confident conclusions\n- Missing sensitivity analyses: reward weight schedules, learning rate, LoRA rank\n- Three-way decomposition uses only 60 neutral pairs (underpowered by the paper's own standards)\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/worst/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: adbe9642b04ad0e22 (use SendMessage with to: 'adbe9642b04ad0e22' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 72248\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 165889</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00408ad4-18c8-47de-8763-c4ca6904e068","uuid":"66a6edcb-5878-49e1-83f0-8cb12c4d0db7","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:51:04.520Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Representation-Level Rewards Are Not Enough: Why Internal Sycophancy Detection Fails to Transfer to Training\"\n\n**Core question:** Can activation-space sycophancy directions serve as reward signals for GRPO training?\n\n**Method (DC-GRPO):** Combines three reward components:\n- R1: negative projection onto sycophancy direction d_syc (computed as mean difference of hidden states between sycophantic and genuine responses)\n- R2: rank-based contrastive reward comparing relative positions along d_syc within a batch\n- R3: NLI-based behavioral reward measuring output consistency with provided evidence\n- Composite reward: R = w1*R1 + w2*R2 + w3*R3 (final weights: 0.3, 0.2, 0.5)\n- Trained Qwen3-8B with LoRA (rank 64) for 6000 steps\n- d_syc recalibrated every 500 steps when cosine similarity drops below 0.7\n\n**Calibration data:** 200 pairs (80 weak + 60 medium + 60 strong evidence), 5-fold CV, optimal layer L24\n\n**Baselines:** R3-only GRPO, ACT-style (L2 activation consistency), Binary Probe (linear classifier on activations), Vanilla Qwen3-8B\n\n**Key results (all negative):**\n1. d_syc AUROC = 0.753 overall; gradient by evidence strength: weak 0.811 → medium 0.724 → strong 0.698\n2. Cross-model transfer fails: Qwen3→Llama3 direction cosine only 0.23, AUROC 0.571 (near random)\n3. Three-way decomposition: d_gen vs d_syc cosine = -0.82 (near-antiparallel)\n4. Direction instability: cosine to initial drops from 0.91 to 0.53 over 6000 steps; 4 recalibrations triggered; held-out AUROC degrades from 0.753 to 0.642\n5. R1-R3 correlation degrades from 0.67 to 0.29 during training\n6. R1-R3 disagreement only 3.8% overall (below 5% threshold); of 50 sampled R1-syc/R3-ok cases, only 16% clear masked sycophancy\n7. R1-only ablation: high R1 reward (0.72) but worst behavioral outcomes (NoF 0.361, MMLU -3.7pp), 34 reward hacking instances\n8. Full evaluation (Table 14): DC-GRPO ranks LAST among trained methods on NoF (0.318), MMLU (0.598), MT-Bench (6.84). No pairwise comparison reaches p<0.05.\n9. Binary Probe matches or exceeds DC-GRPO on every metric with fewer moving parts\n10. All methods reduce warranted revision (from 0.583 vanilla to 0.498-0.538), double-bind unresolved\n11. Turn depth analysis: DC-GRPO shows no differential advantage at any depth; gap with R3-only consistent (1.5-2.1pp)\n\n**Metrics:** NoF (↓), ToF (↑), Warranted Revision Rate (↑), Unwarranted Capitulation Rate (↓), RC-AUC (↑), SycophancyEval (↑), MMLU, MT-Bench\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (what was planned)\n\n13 experiments were planned:\n- exp-001: Calibration data expansion (200 pairs, AUROC by evidence strength/layer) — COVERED in paper (Tables 1-2)\n- exp-002: DC-GRPO main training — COVERED (Table 5)\n- exp-003: R3-only baseline — COVERED (Table 6)\n- exp-004: ACT-style baseline — COVERED (Table 14)\n- exp-005: Full evaluation across all benchmarks — COVERED (Tables 14-16)\n- exp-006: Direction stability monitoring — COVERED (Table 7)\n- exp-007: R1-R3 disagreement analysis — COVERED (Tables 8-10)\n- exp-008: Binary probe baseline — COVERED (Table 17)\n- exp-009: R1-only ablation — COVERED (Table 11)\n- exp-010: Turn depth analysis — COVERED (Tables 12-13)\n- exp-011: R1+R2 no R3 ablation — SKIPPED (conditional on exp-009 showing ≥5pp improvement)\n- exp-012: Cross-model transfer — COVERED (Table 3)\n- exp-013: Three-way neutral baseline — COVERED (Table 4)\n\nThe paper appears to cover all non-skipped experiments.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions in detail:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the DC-GRPO framework well-designed? Are the reward components well-motivated? Is the experimental setup (model choice, LoRA config, training steps, recalibration strategy) reasonable?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments comprehensive? Are baselines appropriate and fairly compared? Is statistical testing adequate (bootstrap tests, p-values)? Are there missing experiments or controls?\n\n3. **Analysis quality**: Are the mechanistic explanations for failure convincing? Is the evidence-strength confound analysis well-executed? Is the direction stability analysis informative? Is the R1-R3 disagreement analysis well-designed?\n\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce the experiments? Are hyperparameters, data construction, and evaluation protocols clearly described?\n\n5. **Presentation of results**: Are tables and figures informative? Is the paper well-organized? Are claims supported by data?\n\n6. **Weaknesses specific to methods/experiments**: What are the key methodological limitations? What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n\nProvide your review in this format:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Minor Issues\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n","agentId":"adbe9642b04ad0e22","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**Score: 4 (Weak Accept) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\nThe review evaluates the \"worst\" scenario paper (negative results) across all requested dimensions:\n\n**Key strengths identified:**\n- Evidence-strength stratification (AUROC gradient from 0.811 weak to 0.698 strong) is a genuine methodological contribution that exposes confounds in prior work\n- Direction stability analysis with cosine tracking, held-out AUROC, and recalibration events is thorough\n- R1-only ablation demonstrating reward hacking (34 instances) is a valuable cautionary finding\n- Statistical testing is honest: all pairwise p-values reported, no cherry-picking\n- Comprehensive baseline coverage spanning the relevant design space\n- Three-way decomposition (cosine = -0.82) provides structural geometric insight\n\n**Key weaknesses identified:**\n- Single model (Qwen3-8B) with single LoRA config; the \"fundamental limitation\" claim is overstated for n=1\n- Recalibration strategy is underexplored (only one approach tested), so direction instability could be an engineering problem rather than inherent\n- R1-R3 disagreement analysis has circularity: low disagreement could reflect R1's degraded accuracy rather than absence of masked sycophancy\n- Manual review sample too small (n=50, only 8 positives) for confident conclusions\n- Missing sensitivity analyses: reward weight schedules, learning rate, LoRA rank\n- Three-way decomposition uses only 60 neutral pairs (underpowered by the paper's own standards)\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T110318/worst/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":165889,"totalTokens":72248,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3848,"cache_read_input_tokens":67960,"output_tokens":439,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3848},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":439,"cache_read_input_tokens":67960,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3848,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3848,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":2,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a394509a943817adf","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RWbnaMXHnZujaHQMESZFFb","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":124695,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":186783},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"83db2272-79ba-4081-8e67-c144adbbb82e","session_id":"00408ad4-18c8-47de-8763-c4ca6904e068"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EuY9UuHjE3CNMpAiHyjCGv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DW1fRqTB4nd7cjmHkt4oTv","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260518T110318\ntier: worst\noverall_score: 3/6 Weak Reject\ntimestamp: 2026-05-18T12:15:00+00:00\n---\n\n# Dream Review Report: Representation-Level Rewards Are Not Enough\n\n**Paper**: \"Representation-Level Rewards Are Not Enough: Why Internal Sycophancy Detection Fails to Transfer to Training\"\n**Reviewer**: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n**Review Mode**: Full review, worst-tier Dream scenario\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether activation-space sycophancy directions can serve as reward signals for GRPO training, and reports a negative result: Direction-Contrastive GRPO (DC-GRPO) provides no statistically significant improvement over a behavioral-reward-only baseline at 8B scale. The paper diagnoses three failure modes (evidence-strength confounds in the sycophancy direction, direction instability under LoRA training, and the near-antiparallel geometry of sycophantic vs. genuine revision directions). While the intellectual honesty is commendable and the diagnostic analysis contains genuinely useful observations, the novelty of these findings is limited, the related work coverage has significant gaps, and the scope of evidence (single model, single scale, single training method) constrains the generalizability of the negative conclusions.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Intellectual honesty about negative results.** The paper does not attempt to salvage a failed method through selective reporting. All three original claims are honestly evaluated against pre-registered thresholds (e.g., the 5% disagreement threshold for Claim 3), and each is clearly reported as failing. The progression from the MVP AUROC of 0.826 to the expanded 0.753 is transparently documented with the explanation that the MVP was inflated by weak-evidence-only sampling. This level of transparency is rare and valuable.\n\n- **The evidence-strength confound analysis is the paper's strongest contribution.** The stratification of AUROC by evidence strength (0.811 weak, 0.724 medium, 0.698 strong) reveals that d_syc partially encodes \"hesitation in the face of weak evidence\" rather than sycophantic cognition per se. This finding has implications beyond this paper: it suggests that existing probing studies reporting high AUROCs for sycophancy detection (e.g., >0.97 in \"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\") may be inflated by uncontrolled evidence-strength confounds. This is a methodological contribution to the representation probing literature.\n\n- **Direction stability tracking is thorough and informative.** The sawtooth pattern of cosine decay with recalibration attempts (Table 9), the quantified drift rate of 0.062 cosine/1000 steps, and the parallel AUROC degradation from 0.753 to 0.642 provide concrete empirical evidence that representation-level reward signals face a fundamental stationarity problem during online training. This observation generalizes to any method (CRAFT, Linear Probe Penalties, etc.) that uses internal model states as training signals.\n\n- **The three-way decomposition finding (d_gen vs. d_syc cosine = -0.82) is conceptually important.** It suggests that, at the linear probing level, sycophancy and genuine revision are not independent constructs but rather opposite poles of a single \"being persuaded\" axis. This challenges the foundational assumption of directional decomposition approaches and raises the question of whether non-linear methods might fare better.\n\n- **Comprehensive baselines.** The inclusion of R3-only, ACT-style, Binary Probe, and Vanilla baselines, all with identical hyperparameters and paired bootstrap significance tests, provides a clean experimental framework. The null result is convincing precisely because the comparison is fair.\n\n- **The R1-only ablation revealing reward hacking is diagnostic.** The finding that R1 reward reaches 0.72 while behavioral metrics worsen (NoF 0.361, worst among all methods) is a clean demonstration of Goodhart's Law applied to representation-level rewards, with concrete examples of the hacking pattern (lengthy hedging prefixes that project away from d_syc without reducing sycophancy).\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Critical\n\n1. **The negative result is under-scoped and over-generalized.** The title claims \"Representation-Level Rewards Are Not Enough\" but the evidence comes from one model (Qwen3-8B), one scale (8B), one training method (LoRA rank 64), one direction extraction method (mean difference vector, not PCA or CCS), and one set of reward weights (0.3/0.2/0.5). The paper acknowledges this in the Limitations section but the title and abstract framing do not adequately qualify these constraints. The strongest defensible claim is \"DC-GRPO with mean-difference d_syc does not improve over R3-only GRPO on Qwen3-8B with LoRA,\" which is substantially narrower than the paper's framing. A negative result paper must be especially careful about the scope of its negative claims, because over-generalization risks discouraging productive research directions on insufficient evidence.\n\n2. **Missing foundational references severely weaken the positioning.** The paper fails to cite several works that are directly relevant to its methodology and findings:\n   - **Representation Engineering (Zou et al., 2023)** and **Contrast-Consistent Search (Burns et al., 2023)**: The d_syc extraction procedure is a direct instance of these frameworks. Their absence makes the paper appear unaware of its own methodological lineage.\n   - **Activation Addition / Inference-Time Intervention (Turner et al., 2023; Li et al., 2023)**: Relevant to the discussion of why discriminative directions may not serve as effective interventional signals.\n   - **Non-identifiability of steering vectors (Venkatesh & Kurapath, 2026; Tan et al., 2024)**: The finding that cross-model direction cosine is only 0.23 and that directions are model-specific echoes this literature on steering vector non-identifiability and OOD degradation, but it goes entirely uncited.\n   - **RLHF foundational works (Ouyang et al., 2022; Bai et al., 2022; Rafailov et al., 2023)**: Standard citations for any paper discussing reward signals for LLM training.\n   - **\"Sycophancy Hides Linearly in the Attention Heads\" (2026)**: Directly relevant to the question of where sycophancy signals live in the network and their relationship to truthfulness directions.\n   - **\"Sycophantic Anchors\" (2026)**: Relevant to the mechanistic trace analysis and the representation-behavioral gap.\n   - **INTUITOR (2026)**: Validates internal-signal GRPO, directly relevant as a comparison point.\n   \n   With only ~15 references total, the bibliography is thin for a paper that positions itself as establishing \"empirical boundary conditions\" for an entire class of methods. A characterization paper of this ambition should cite 30-40 works to demonstrate comprehensive understanding of the landscape it claims to bound.\n\n3. **The novelty of each individual finding is limited.** Taking each diagnostic contribution in isolation:\n   - *Evidence-strength confounds*: That probing accuracy depends on the difficulty of the discrimination task is not surprising. The specific quantification (0.811 to 0.698 gradient) is useful but the conceptual insight is expected.\n   - *Direction instability under training*: That LoRA modifies representations and therefore pre-computed directions drift is a predictable consequence of the setup. The paper does not explore obvious mitigations (freezing the extraction layer, using the reward model's activations instead, online direction estimation with moving windows, or reducing LoRA rank at the extraction layer).\n   - *d_gen approximately equals -d_syc*: This is interesting but tested with only 60 neutral pairs on one model. The cosine of -0.82 leaves 33% unexplained variance in the orthogonal component, which the paper does not explore.\n   - *No incremental value of R1*: Given the above two findings (noisy direction + direction drift), the null training result is the expected consequence rather than an independent finding.\n\n   The three findings are not independent; they form a causal chain (confounded direction + direction drift = noisy reward = null training result). Framing them as three separate contributions overstates the paper's contribution count.\n\n### Major\n\n4. **Alternative explanations for the null result are insufficiently explored.** The paper concludes that representation-level rewards fundamentally fail, but several alternative explanations remain untested:\n   - The reward weights (0.3/0.2/0.5) are presented without sensitivity analysis. With w1=0.3 and documented R1 degradation, the effective contribution of R1 in the second half of training is negligible. Higher initial w1 with a decay schedule, or adaptive weighting based on R1-R3 correlation, might yield different results.\n   - LoRA rank 64 constrains representation space changes but also constrains the model's ability to learn from R1 signal. The interaction between LoRA rank and direction stability is not analyzed.\n   - The recalibration threshold of 0.7 was triggered only 4 times. More aggressive recalibration (e.g., every 200 steps regardless of cosine) or online direction estimation using recent rollouts is not tested.\n   - The direction is computed as a mean difference vector. PCA-based extraction (top principal component of difference vectors, as used in the best-tier dream) or CCS-based unsupervised extraction might yield more robust directions.\n\n5. **The human annotation for masked sycophancy is weak.** 50 samples with no reported inter-annotator agreement, no annotation guidelines, and a three-way categorization (clear/ambiguous/false positive) that is itself subjective. The 16% \"clear masked sycophancy\" rate (8 out of 50) has a 95% CI of approximately [7%, 29%] by the binomial proportion interval, meaning the true rate is highly uncertain. This analysis supports neither a strong positive nor a strong negative claim about masked sycophancy.\n\n6. **The paper does not adequately distinguish its diagnostic contributions from what is already known or easily predicted.** For the paper to succeed as a characterization study, the findings must be genuinely surprising or non-obvious to the community. The paper should explicitly argue: \"Prior to this work, a reasonable researcher would have expected X, but we found Y.\" For direction instability, the paper does not establish that the community expected directions to be stable under LoRA training. For the evidence-strength confound, the paper does not show that prior probing studies (e.g., \"Sycophancy Is Not One Thing\" with AUROC >0.97) controlled for evidence strength, making it unclear whether this is a new finding or a replication of a known limitation.\n\n### Minor\n\n7. **The Related Work section (page 2-3) is structured as four short paragraphs rather than a synthesized narrative.** Each paragraph summarizes a cluster of papers but does not clearly position the current work's diagnostic contribution relative to each. The section reads as a compressed survey rather than an argument for why this specific investigation is needed.\n\n8. **Several citations appear to be synthetic or unverifiable.** References like \"Wei et al. (2024) Truth Decay,\" \"Sun et al. (2024) SMART,\" and \"Chen et al. (2024) ACT\" use generic author lists and venues that do not match verifiable publications. In a final submission, every citation must be traceable to a real paper. (This may be an artifact of the dream simulation, but it must be flagged.)\n\n9. **The paper lacks a formal definition of several metrics.** Revision-Capitulation AUC (RC-AUC) is used throughout but not formally defined. NoF is described as \"frequency of unwarranted opinion flips\" but the operationalization (how \"unwarranted\" is determined, the NLI threshold, etc.) is left to the benchmark reference.\n\n10. **No code or data release is mentioned.** The Conclusion states \"We release our expanded calibration dataset and direction stability monitoring code\" but no URL or repository is provided. For a negative-result paper, reproducibility is especially important.\n\n11. **The paper uses en-dashes in ranges (e.g., \"L16--19\") throughout, which is correct for number ranges, but several em-dash-like constructions appear in the text (e.g., \"even when their initial answer was correct\" constructions). A final proofread for AI writing markers is warranted.**\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **What happens with higher R1 weight early in training and a decay schedule?** If R1 contributes useful signal early (when direction is fresh, cosine >0.9) but noise later, an adaptive weighting scheme that reduces w1 as R1-R3 correlation drops could potentially rescue the approach. Was this tested?\n\n2. **Would freezing the LoRA adapter at the direction extraction layer (L24) while training other layers stabilize d_syc?** This would constrain representation drift at the critical layer while allowing the model to learn anti-sycophancy behavior at other layers.\n\n3. **How does the result change if d_syc is extracted via PCA on difference vectors (as in the best-tier scenario) rather than mean difference?** The mean difference and top-PCA-component can diverge substantially when the contrastive pairs have heterogeneous activation patterns.\n\n4. **What is the AUROC of a non-linear probe (e.g., 2-layer MLP) on the same data?** If a non-linear probe achieves substantially higher AUROC, the failure is specific to linear methods, not representation-level rewards in general.\n\n5. **The three-way decomposition uses only 60 neutral pairs. How sensitive is the d_gen-d_syc cosine of -0.82 to the neutral condition definition and sample size?** With 60 pairs, the bootstrap CI on this cosine could be wide.\n\n6. **Would using the reward model's activations (as in Linear Probe Penalties) instead of the policy model's avoid the direction instability problem?** The reward model is frozen during training, so its directions would not drift.\n\n---\n\n## Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses an important question (can representation-level sycophancy detection transfer to training?) and reports its negative findings honestly. The evidence-strength confound analysis and direction stability tracking are genuinely useful empirical contributions. However, the paper falls below the acceptance threshold for three reasons: (1) the negative result is under-scoped (single model, single scale, single training variant) yet over-generalized in its framing, risking premature closure of a research direction; (2) the related work coverage has critical gaps that undermine the paper's claim to establish \"boundary conditions\" for representation-level reward methods; (3) the individual diagnostic findings, while useful, are largely predictable consequences of the setup rather than genuinely surprising insights. The paper would be strengthened by testing at least one obvious mitigation (adaptive weighting, online direction estimation, non-linear probes) to demonstrate that the failure is robust rather than an artifact of design choices, and by substantially expanding the reference coverage.\n\n**Confidence: 4/5**. I am confident in the assessment of novelty limitations and related work gaps. The experimental methodology appears sound, though the dream-simulated nature of the results introduces uncertainty about whether real experiments would match these projections.\n\n---\n\n## Path to Acceptance\n\nThe paper could reach 4/6 (Weak Accept) at EACL analysis track with:\n\n1. **Qualify the negative claim scope.** Title and abstract must make clear this is \"at 8B scale with LoRA and mean-difference direction extraction,\" not a general statement about representation-level rewards.\n2. **Test at least 2-3 obvious mitigations** (adaptive R1 weighting, online direction estimation, non-linear probing) to demonstrate the failure is robust. If any mitigation works, the paper becomes stronger as a \"here is what you need to do\" paper rather than \"this cannot work.\"\n3. **Expand references to 30+.** Cite Representation Engineering, CCS, Activation Addition, non-identifiability literature, RLHF foundations, and recent mechanistic sycophancy work.\n4. **Expand the three-way decomposition.** The d_gen-d_syc cosine finding is the most conceptually interesting result but rests on 60 pairs from one model. Replicate on Llama-3-8B and test sensitivity to neutral condition construction.\n5. **Improve human annotation.** Expand from 50 to 150+ samples, report inter-annotator agreement, document annotation guidelines.\n6. **Formally define all metrics** (RC-AUC, NoF operationalization, \"masked sycophancy\" criteria) in the main text.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Novelty | 2/4 |\n| Significance | 2.5/4 |\n| Soundness | 3/4 |\n| Presentation | 2.5/4 |\n| Overall | 3/6 (Weak Reject) |\n| Confidence | 4/5 |","project":"contrastive_entrain_calib","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260518T110318","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":687,"cache_read_input_tokens":123575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":687,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RWbnaMXHnZujaHQMESZFFb","session_id":"00408ad4-18c8-47de-8763-c4ca6904e068","uuid":"2f8b026c-a76f-408b-a80e-0bdb08